---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-vs-vitamin-d3`;

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'What is the difference between vitamin D and vitamin D3?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: '"Vitamin D" is an umbrella term for a family of secosteroid compounds. Vitamin D3 (cholecalciferol) is the specific form produced in animal skin from 7-dehydrocholesterol and found in fatty fish and egg yolk. Vitamin D2 (ergocalciferol) is the plant/fungal form. On a supplement label, "Vitamin D" almost always means D3 unless it explicitly says D2 or ergocalciferol.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'Which is better: vitamin D or vitamin D3?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'The relevant comparison is D3 versus D2. Meta-analysis (Tripkovic 2012) shows cholecalciferol (D3) raises serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D about 60â€“90% more effectively than ergocalciferol (D2) at equivalent oral doses. D3 is the form your skin makes and the form most modern guidelines recommend as a supplement.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'Are vitamin D and cholecalciferol the same thing?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Cholecalciferol is the biochemical name for vitamin D3. So when a supplement label lists "cholecalciferol," you\'re taking vitamin D3. The umbrella term "vitamin D" includes both D3 and D2 (ergocalciferol).' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'Is D2 vegan?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Yes â€” vitamin D2 (ergocalciferol) is produced by UV-irradiating ergosterol in yeasts or fungi and is suitable for vegans. Most vitamin D3 supplements come from lanolin (sheep wool), but plant-based D3 from lichen is also available.' } },
  ],
};
const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D vs Vitamin D3',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D vs Vitamin D3', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D vs Vitamin D3: What's the Difference? (Cholecalciferol vs Ergocalciferol)"
  description="Vitamin D3 (cholecalciferol) vs vitamin D2 (ergocalciferol) â€” how the two forms differ in origin, absorption, and effectiveness at raising serum 25(OH)D."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

    <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-vs-vitamin-d3", label: "D vs D3" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />


  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D vs Vitamin D<sub>3</sub></h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      "Vitamin D" on a supplement label is almost always vitamin D<sub>3</sub> (cholecalciferol), but
      "vitamin D" as a chemistry term is an umbrella including both D<sub>3</sub> and D<sub>2</sub>
      (ergocalciferol). Here's what actually differs.
    </p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl shadow-sm mb-10">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-gray-50 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-200">
          <tr>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-3">Attribute</th>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-3">Vitamin D<sub>3</sub> (cholecalciferol)</th>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-3">Vitamin D<sub>2</sub> (ergocalciferol)</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="text-gray-700">
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Origin</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Animal skin from 7-DHC + UVB; fatty fish; egg yolk</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Yeasts/fungi from ergosterol + UV; UV-exposed mushrooms</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Chemistry</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Side chain has H at C24</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Side chain has methyl at C24; double bond at C22â€“C23</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Effect on serum 25(OH)D</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Raises by ~1 ng/mL per 100 IU/day</td><td class="px-4 py-3">~60â€“90% less effective per equivalent dose (Tripkovic 2012)</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Half-life of the metabolite</td><td class="px-4 py-3">25(OH)D<sub>3</sub> ~15â€“25 days apparent; ~60 d terminal</td><td class="px-4 py-3">25(OH)D<sub>2</sub> shorter</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Common supplement source</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Lanolin (sheep wool) or lichen (vegan)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Yeast or fungi (vegan)</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Prescription forms</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Most European &amp; ROW prescription vitamin D</td><td class="px-4 py-3">US prescription 50,000 IU capsules (Drisdol)</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Recommendation</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Preferred by most modern guidelines</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Reasonable for vegans who avoid lichen-D<sub>3</sub>; ask about substitution</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Why the effectiveness gap?</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      Both forms are 25-hydroxylated by CYP2R1 in the liver. But D<sub>2</sub> is less avidly bound
      by vitamin D-binding protein (DBP), cleared faster, and its metabolites are catabolised more
      rapidly. Net result: D<sub>2</sub> produces smaller and shorter-lived rises in serum 25(OH)D.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      If your prescription is ergocalciferol (Drisdol, "vitamin D2"), it's worth asking your
      prescriber whether cholecalciferol at the same IU dose would suit you better â€” most will
      say yes.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Reading a supplement label</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>"Vitamin D3"</strong> or <strong>"cholecalciferol"</strong> â€” the effective form.</li>
      <li><strong>"Vitamin D2"</strong> or <strong>"ergocalciferol"</strong> â€” less potent but still works; usually cheaper and vegan.</li>
      <li><strong>"Vitamin D"</strong> without a number â€” check the ingredients panel. Almost always D<sub>3</sub> in modern supplements.</li>
      <li>Doses are given in IU or Âµg (1 Âµg = 40 IU). See our <a href="/vitamin-d-unit-converter" class="text-amber-700 underline">unit converter</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-vs-vitamin-d3" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
